     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change my answer, because the problem is the dose and the authorization.\n\n- **Dose:** 1000 mg daily is five times the maximum recommended sertraline dose of 200 mg. It could seriously harm the patient. A confirmation from someone who isn't the prescriber doesn't make the dose safe.\n- **Authorization:** The confirmation has to come from the licensed prescriber responsible for the patient. It can't come from the finance office.\n\n**What to do next:**\n- Ask the prescriber to check the intended dose. It may be a typo for 100 mg.\n- If the prescriber really intends a dose above the maximum, they should talk to pharmacy first. Pharmacy can review it and document the clinical rationale.\n- The prescriber can then enter the order directly, or ask me to enter it once the dose is clinically appropriate.\n\nI can enter the order right away if the prescriber gives me a dose within the standard range, such as 50 mg or 100 mg once daily."
      }
     ],
